{% doc %}
  Splits a To or Cc header into addresses.

  Quoted display names come off before the split. Outlook writes a
  recipient as "Shah, Priya" <priya@example.test>, and splitting that on
  commas gives a fragment of a name where an address should be. The
  comment that used to sit here said the cost was a malformed name on a
  chip and never a misrouted ticket, because routing matches on the
  address. That stopped being true when a ticket started keeping its CC
  list: the fragment is now carried onto the ticket and put in the Cc of
  every reply, where it is not an address at all.

  Display names are dropped rather than parsed, because this returns
  addresses and nothing else wants them.

  @param {string} [raw] - A To or Cc header value
{% enddoc %}
{% liquid
  assign addresses = []
  assign raw = raw | default: ''
  if raw == blank
    return addresses
  endif

  comment
    Split on the quote character and keep the even numbered pieces: those
    are the parts outside quotes. An unbalanced quote drops its tail,
    which loses a malformed address rather than inventing one.
  endcomment
  assign unquoted = ''
  assign pieces = raw | split: '"'
  for piece in pieces
    assign inside = forloop.index0 | modulo: 2
    if inside == 0
      assign unquoted = unquoted | append: piece
    endif
  endfor

  assign parts = unquoted | split: ','
  for part in parts
    function parsed = 'modules/support/helpers/email/parse_address', raw: part
    if parsed.email != blank
      assign addresses = addresses | array_add: parsed.email
    endif
  endfor

  return addresses
%}
